from editorial_authoring_002_184 import append
append(r'''
[171]
@Behavior measurement|Time, uncertainty and interaction|intermediate
Why can the same person prefer an immediate reward in one task and a delayed reward in another?|Compare delay length, amount, credibility, liquidity constraints and repeated choices in hypothetical discounting tasks; distinguish measured preferences from a fixed character trait.|1
How can two hazards with similar estimated probabilities evoke different levels of concern?|Compare familiarity, control, dread, trust, exposure and communication in labeled fictional hazards; distinguish perceived risk from a calculation of expected loss.|3
How do repeated encounters change cooperation when people remember earlier choices?|Compare one-shot and repeated games, feedback, reputation, exit and institutional rules; do not infer universal human motives from a laboratory game.|6
What changes when a group can reward cooperation or punish a broken rule?|Compare who defines the norm, enforcement cost, legitimacy, mistaken punishment and unequal power; distinguish compliance from agreement.|5
@Behavior measurement|Observation and interpretation|advanced
How can participants change their behavior because they believe a researcher is watching?|Compare direct observation, demand cues, novelty, privacy expectations and habituation in ethically approved designs; distinguish an observer effect from an assumed universal Hawthorne effect.|8
[172]
[173]
[174]
@Later-life variation|Cognition and household change|intermediate
Why can age-related changes in processing speed coexist with stable knowledge in the same person?|Compare cognitive domains, repeated testing, sensory access, education, health and within-person change; separate population patterns from individual diagnosis.|2
How does an older household reorganize daily support after a partner dies or relatives move away?|Trace household composition, chosen support, housing, finances and formal services in consented or fictional cases; avoid assuming co-residence guarantees care.|5
[175]
@Children's institutions|War, welfare and adolescence|intermediate
How can a child's wartime diary record both ordinary routines and disruptions adults describe differently?|Compare a named diary's dates, intended audience, censorship, preservation and adult accounts; do not treat one child's testimony as typical of all wartime childhoods.|3
How did responsibility for children move between families, charities and welfare agencies in one historical jurisdiction?|Trace dated eligibility, referral, supervision, family separation, institutional records and children's accounts; distinguish stated protection from actual experiences.|4
When did adolescence become a distinct category in one society's schools, work rules and popular media?|Compare age thresholds, education, employment, household responsibilities and uneven class or gender adoption; avoid projecting a single modern stage onto every society.|6
[176]
@Youth institutions|Organizing and gaming communities|intermediate
How did a student campaign turn informal peer networks into a durable political organization?|Trace meetings, publications, representation, funding, authorities' responses and disagreement through one dated campaign; distinguish youth participants from adult accounts about them.|5
How does a local gaming community organize belonging beyond playing the same game?|Compare tournaments, moderation, language, venues, costs, gendered access and offline relationships in a consented community; separate community practices from game lore or design.|6
[177]
@Collective action|Anticolonial and labor organization|advanced
How did women organize an anticolonial campaign beyond the leaders most often commemorated?|Trace logistics, underground communications, fundraising, care work, imprisonment and disagreements within a named movement; compare contemporary accounts with later national memory.|2
What changed when women in a particular industry organized over conditions that a union's existing leadership overlooked?|Compare recruitment, strike demands, pay structures, care responsibilities, representation and documented outcomes; retain differences among workers rather than treating gender as their only shared interest.|3
[178]
@Institutions and vulnerability|Schooling and access to support|advanced
How did a boys' school teach ideals of manhood through its timetable, discipline and extracurricular life?|Compare prospectuses, rules, pupil accounts, class access and dissent in one dated institution; separate prescribed ideals from what boys actually experienced.|5
What does research on men's help-seeking measure when access barriers and masculine norms occur together?|Compare service availability, cost, privacy, survey wording, stigma, age and occupation; do not infer reluctance or a diagnosis from gender alone.|8
[179]
@Rights and historical authorship|Education, transport and interpretation|advanced
How did disabled pupils and families contest the boundary between separate and inclusive schooling?|Trace one jurisdiction's rules, access, support, testimony, language and differing community positions; distinguish institutional labels from educational experiences.|3
How did disabled activists turn an inaccessible bus system into a public transport rights campaign?|Compare vehicle design, routes, protests, negotiations, costs and implementation in a named documented campaign; distinguish adopting a rule from enabling reliable journeys.|5
How does a disabled historian's choice of questions change the interpretation of an institutional archive?|Compare authorship, community vocabulary, source selection, missing testimony and interpretive disagreement through attributed published work; avoid assigning one viewpoint to all disabled scholars.|7
[180]
@Health-system access|Workforce geography and outcome measures|advanced
Why can training more clinicians leave rural communities with limited access to care?|Trace recruitment, retention, housing, equipment, supervision, referral and family needs in a selected system; distinguish workforce headcount from available clinical services.|6
What can a preventable-hospitalization indicator tell us about primary care and what can it not attribute to it?|Compare condition definitions, coding, severity, admission thresholds, deprivation and access using a fictional dataset; avoid treating an ecological indicator as an individual clinical judgment.|7
[181]
@Hospital coordination|Theatres, infection prevention and representation|intermediate
Why can an operating theatre schedule fail even when a room appears free?|Trace staffing, instruments, anesthesia, recovery capacity, emergency demand and cancellations in a hypothetical day; describe coordination without clinical operating instructions.|2
What does an infection-prevention team coordinate across wards, laboratories and hospital facilities?|Map surveillance definitions, cleaning, training, supplies, feedback and investigation responsibilities; distinguish a systems explanation from patient-specific infection advice.|6
How should a hospital film mark a reconstruction so viewers do not mistake it for an actual patient's clinical footage?|Plan labels, actors' consent, composite cases, medical records, chronology and visual boundaries; preserve uncertainty and exclude identifiable patient information.|9
[182]
@Emergency-service learning|Dispatcher training evaluation|advanced
How can a dispatcher training program demonstrate improvement beyond trainees remembering a checklist?|Compare simulated calls, location accuracy, accessibility, workload, retention, feedback and independently defined outcomes; exclude operational scripts or replacing official emergency instructions.|8
[183]
@Prevention design|Roads, schools and public events|advanced
How can a road-injury prevention evaluation separate safer travel from simply fewer journeys?|Compare exposure by transport mode, speed environment, severity, reporting, nearby displacement and before-after controls; reserve design prescriptions for qualified local authorities.|2
What should a school emergency plan test about reunification, accessibility and communication beyond a timed evacuation drill?|Map age, disability access, guardianship, languages, substitute staff, privacy and follow-up responsibility at a governance level; exclude facility vulnerabilities or tactical procedures.|5
What assumptions can make a public-event evacuation model look more reliable than it is?|Compare attendance, mobility variation, route availability, behavior, uncertainty and validation against appropriately obtained observations; exclude real-event operating capacities or certification.|6
[184]
@Fire systems and investigation|Fuel, water, communication and evacuation|advanced
Why does wildfire fuel moisture require more than a single weather reading to interpret?|Compare live and dead vegetation, fuel size, drying history, sampling, local variation and model uncertainty; exclude ignition experiments or suppression tactics.|2
Why can a hydrant's location fail to reveal the water supply available during a fire?|Explain network pressure, simultaneous demand, maintenance, storage and professional testing conceptually; exclude testing instructions, site capacity declarations or infrastructure vulnerability mapping.|5
How can a fire incident review distinguish a radio equipment problem from a communication-process problem?|Compare equipment records, message clarity, interoperability, acknowledgement, workload and recorded information available then; exclude frequencies, operational vulnerabilities or tactical direction.|6
What can investigators learn when evacuation instructions, building conditions and occupants' actions diverged during a fire?|Compare dated communications, accessibility, compartmentation, witness uncertainty and official findings; avoid hindsight blame or supplying public guidance that conflicts with official local advice.|9
''')
